---
import LearnLayout from "../../components/learn/LearnLayout.astro";
import LearnNav from "../../components/learn/LearnNav.astro";
import { haloCold, r9700Fn } from "../../data/release041";
import { tagBlob, RELEASE_URL, RELEASE_CHANGELOG } from "../../data/learn";

const description =
  "How hipfire 0.4.1 runs native multi-token-prediction speculative decoding on Flash-Next: greedy and lossless sampled verification, penalties on every verify row, measured RDNA verify costs, n-gram takeovers and the AR floor.";

// Window cost in ms for 1..8 verify rows: the measured fits hipfire 0.4.1 prices
// n-gram takeovers with (crates/hipfire-arch-qwen4/src/mtp_spec.rs, ngram_window_cost).
const halo = [30.5, 37.4, 44.3, 51.2, 58.1, 65.0, 71.9, 78.8];
const r97 = [30.3, 45.1, 59.9, 74.7, 89.5, 104.3, 119.1, 133.9];
const maxMs = 140;
const plotH = 200;
const x0 = 64;
const groupW = 66;
const yOf = (ms: number) => 236 - (ms / maxMs) * plotH;
const ticks = [0, 35, 70, 105, 140];
const breakEven = (row: number[], i: number) => (row[i] / row[0]).toFixed(2);
---

<LearnLayout title="Speculative decoding on RDNA" description={description}>
  <article class="distil">
    <div class="distil-meta"><a href="/learn">/learn</a> &middot; Flash-Next &middot; native MTP</div>
    <h1 class="distil-title">Speculative decoding on RDNA</h1>
    <p class="distil-lede">
      Decoding one token at a time leaves most of a GPU idle. Speculative decoding
      guesses several tokens cheaply, then checks all of them with one pass of the
      real model. In 0.4.1, hipfire does this on Qwen3.8 Flash-Next with the
      model's own <strong>multi-token-prediction (MTP) head</strong> as the guesser,
      for greedy <em>and</em> sampled requests, with the request's penalties applied
      on every row it checks.
    </p>
    <p class="distil-lede">
      This page explains the idea, the parts that are specific to RDNA (what a
      verify row actually costs on Strix Halo and on the Radeon AI PRO R9700),
      and what it measured in the 0.4.1 release.
    </p>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">01</span> Why decode leaves room</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">the idea</span>
      <p>
        Generating one token means reading every active weight once and doing
        very little math with each byte. That is a bandwidth-bound job: the
        GPU's matrix units wait on memory. If you hand the same pass
        <em>several</em> positions at once, the weights are read once and used
        several times, so a few extra rows cost much less than a few extra
        tokens would.
      </p>
      <p>
        Speculative decoding spends that slack. A cheap <strong>draft</strong>
        proposes the next <em>k</em> tokens. The full <strong>target</strong>
        model scores the current position plus all <em>k</em> guesses in one
        <em>verify</em> pass of <em>k</em>&nbsp;+&nbsp;1 rows. Every guess the
        target agrees with is a token you got without its own decode step.
      </p>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; one speculative window, k = 3 drafts, 4 verify rows</div>
        <pre class="ascii-fig">{`  emitted so far:  … the  quick
  draft head:              brown   fox   jumps        (k = 3 guesses)
  verify pass:     row 0   row 1   row 2   row 3      (one target pass, 4 rows)
  target says:     brown   fox     leaps   ·
                   ✓       ✓       ✗ → emit "leaps"
  window result:   brown fox leaps     3 tokens for one verify pass`}</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        The window always emits at least one token (the target's own choice at
        the first disagreement, or a bonus token after the last row when every
        draft is accepted), so a window is never worse than one decode step in
        tokens. Whether it is better in <em>time</em> depends on what the extra
        rows cost. That is the RDNA-specific part (&sect;&nbsp;05).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">02</span> The draft is part of the model</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <p>
        Flash-Next carries a native MTP layer: an extra prediction head that
        reads the trunk's hidden state and proposes the tokens after the next
        one. hipfire attaches it as the drafter, so there is no second model to
        download, load or keep in sync. On the two release cards, Strix Halo
        (<code>gfx1151</code>) and the R9700 (<code>gfx1201</code>), native MTP
        is on by default; the draft depth defaults to 3
        (<code>speculation.mtp_k</code>).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Greedy requests</strong> accept the longest prefix of drafts
        that equal the target's argmax. Without n-gram takeovers (&sect;&nbsp;06),
        0.4.1's greedy MTP and plain AR agree token for token across the
        repaired paths: 2&ndash;8-row verify projections now sum in exactly the
        single-row decode order (the <code>gemv_bf16_xf32_k4_rows_r2..r8</code>
        kernels), and the Q8 recurrent state is quantized at every verify row
        the way AR quantizes it after every token.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">03</span> Sampled requests: rejection sampling</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">the idea</span>
      <p>
        With temperature above zero there is no single &ldquo;right&rdquo; token
        to compare against, so agreement has to be defined on distributions.
        Speculative rejection sampling (Leviathan et&nbsp;al.; Chen et&nbsp;al.)
        does this without changing what you sample from:
      </p>
      <ol class="distil-ol">
        <li>Draw the draft token <code>x</code> from the draft distribution <code>q</code>.</li>
        <li>Accept it with probability <code>min(1, p(x) / q(x))</code>, where <code>p</code> is the target's distribution at that row.</li>
        <li>On rejection, emit a draw from the residual <code>(p &minus; q)<sub>+</sub></code>, renormalized, and end the window.</li>
        <li>If every draft is accepted, draw the bonus token from <code>p</code> at the last row.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The emitted tokens follow <code>p</code> exactly, the same distribution
        plain sampling would use. Drafts only change how many tokens one verify
        pass produces.
      </p>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <p>
        <code>p</code> is <em>exactly</em> the Flash-Next AR sampler's
        distribution: temperature, the request's top-k (absent means 20
        candidates, 0 means the 64-wide pool), min-p and top-p, with the same
        pool, tie order and summation. <code>q</code> applies the same policy to
        the draft head's 8 re-scored candidates and their exact logits. One
        request-seeded random stream supplies every draw, so the same seed
        replays the same tokens.
      </p>
      <div class="lx-note">
        <div class="lx-note-title">lossless, precisely</div>
        <p>
          Sampled MTP matches AR <strong>in distribution</strong>, not byte for
          byte: for a given seed it emits a different (equally valid) token
          stream than AR. The CPU test draws 200,000 trials per case and checks
          the emitted tokens against <code>p</code>; drawing the rejection
          replacement from <code>p</code> instead of the residual fails the same
          test in every control case.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">04</span> Penalties on every verify row</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <p>
        Repeat, presence and frequency penalties depend on history: which tokens
        were already produced. In a speculative window that history grows row
        by row, so each verify row must be penalized against the history
        <em>as if the earlier drafts had been accepted</em>:
      </p>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; the penalty history of verify row i</div>
        <pre class="ascii-fig">{`  H_i = last W tokens of ( prompt ‖ emitted so far ‖ drafts[0 .. i) )

  row 0 penalized by  … prompt, emitted
  row 1 penalized by  … prompt, emitted, d0
  row 2 penalized by  … prompt, emitted, d0, d1
  W = the request's penalty window; a rejected draft never enters the next window's history`}</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        Both <code>p</code> (the target row) and <code>q</code> (the draft step
        proposed for that row) are penalized by the same <code>H_i</code>, so
        rejection sampling still targets the distribution a penalized AR
        sampler would use. Target rows are penalized on the GPU by a prepass
        that is bit-identical to the host penalty stage (same emitted IDs on
        24 of 24 prompts on each architecture), so only the already-penalized
        row is downloaded.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 0.4.0, native MTP verification silently dropped these penalties.
        Agent workloads often send them (the release's agent evaluations
        sample with a presence penalty of 1.5), so penalized requests now
        speculate with the distribution they asked for.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">05</span> What a verify row costs on RDNA</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">the RDNA part</span>
      <p>
        &ldquo;Extra rows are cheap&rdquo; is only true up to a point, and the
        point is different on every card. hipfire 0.4.1 carries
        <strong>measured</strong> window-cost fits for Flash-Next on both
        release cards and uses them to price decisions:
      </p>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; Flash-Next verify window cost by rows, measured fits used by 0.4.1</div>
        <svg class="lx-svg" viewBox="0 0 640 290" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of verify window cost in milliseconds for 1 to 8 rows on Strix Halo and R9700">
          {ticks.map((t) => (
            <g>
              <line x1={x0 - 6} y1={yOf(t)} x2={x0 + groupW * 8} y2={yOf(t)} stroke="#1f1f1f" />
              <text x={x0 - 10} y={yOf(t) + 4} font-size="11" text-anchor="end" class="t-dim">{t}</text>
            </g>
          ))}
          <text x="14" y="140" font-size="11" class="t-dim" transform="rotate(-90 14 140)" text-anchor="middle">ms per window</text>
          {halo.map((v, i) => (
            <g>
              <rect x={x0 + i * groupW + 10} y={yOf(v)} width="20" height={236 - yOf(v)} fill="#e11d2a" />
              <rect x={x0 + i * groupW + 32} y={yOf(r97[i])} width="20" height={236 - yOf(r97[i])} fill="#fbbf24" />
              <text x={x0 + i * groupW + 31} y="254" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" class="t-fg">{i + 1}</text>
            </g>
          ))}
          <text x={x0 + groupW * 4} y="276" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" class="t-dim">verify rows per window (drafts + 1)</text>
          <rect x="420" y="20" width="12" height="12" fill="#e11d2a" />
          <text x="438" y="30" font-size="11" class="t-fg">Strix Halo  30.5 + 6.9 ms/row</text>
          <rect x="420" y="40" width="12" height="12" fill="#fbbf24" />
          <text x="438" y="50" font-size="11" class="t-fg">R9700       30.3 + 14.8 ms/row</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <p>
        One row (a plain decode step) costs about the same on both cards. Each
        extra row adds 6.9&nbsp;ms on Strix Halo but 14.8&nbsp;ms on the R9700.
        Divide a window's cost by one decode step and you get the number of
        tokens the window must emit just to break even with AR:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>drafts</th><th class="num">verify rows</th><th class="num">Halo: tokens to break even</th><th class="num">R9700: tokens to break even</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {[1, 2, 3].map((d) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{d}</td>
                <td class="num">{d + 1}</td>
                <td class="num">{breakEven(halo, d)}</td>
                <td class="num">{breakEven(r97, d)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">
        Ratios computed from the fits above (window cost &divide; one-row cost). Up to 8 rows
        the fits are explicit per-row buckets; 9 or more rows run a different
        expert-sharing route, which 0.4.1 does not extrapolate to and does not
        assume is cheap.
      </p>
      <p>
        This matches the gap between the two cards in &sect;&nbsp;08: the same
        head gains far more where extra rows are cheap. It is also why hipfire chooses the draft depth per
        window: it picks the depth <em>k</em> that maximizes expected emitted
        tokens (1 plus the running product of per-depth acceptance) per unit of
        window cost, and falls back to one-token-per-step when no batched
        window beats it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">06</span> N-gram takeovers, priced on the same clock</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <p>
        Agent and coding traffic repeats itself: file paths, identifiers, JSON
        keys, quoted tool output. A request-local n-gram pool, seeded from the
        full prompt, can propose those continuations for free. In 0.4.1 such a
        match may <em>take over</em> a window from the MTP head (default triple
        5/3/3: match 5 tokens, propose 3).
      </p>
      <p>
        A takeover only happens when its expected emitted tokens per
        millisecond, priced with the same row-cost model as &sect;&nbsp;05, are at
        least what the native window offers at its current acceptance. Verification
        is unchanged: greedy candidates must match the target, and sampled ones
        go through the same rejection test with a point-mass draft
        <code>q = &delta;(candidate)</code> (accept with probability
        <code>p(candidate)</code>, otherwise draw from <code>p</code> with the
        candidate removed). Takeovers are lossless in distribution, but greedy
        text can differ from MTP-only output.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">07</span> The AR floor</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <p>
        Some requests never benefit: low-acceptance prose on a card where rows
        are expensive. The <strong>same-request AR floor</strong> measures
        instead of guessing:
      </p>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; the floor's first windows of a request</div>
        <div class="lx-flow">
          <div class="lx-flow-step"><b>1 · time AR</b><span>one ordinary AR token, timed (sampled draw included), plus one head append</span></div>
          <div class="lx-flow-step"><b>2 · probe</b><span>up to three speculative windows, each one timed</span></div>
          <div class="lx-flow-step"><b>3 · compare</b><span>this request's measured costs per emitted token vs the AR time</span></div>
          <div class="lx-flow-step accent"><b>4 · decide</b><span>nothing strictly cheaper → retire to head-free AR until the next request</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        A tie or an invalid timing also retires. What the floor bounds is the
        speculative <em>probing</em> (one calibration append and at most three
        probe windows before the first measured decision), not the full request
        time. Retired requests are reported as <code>mtp_retired</code> /
        <code>ar_windows</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">08</span> What it buys you</h2>
      <div class="lx-buys">
        <div class="lx-buys-head">Flash-Next decode, 0.4.1 release defaults &middot; medians of three fresh processes</div>
        <div class="lx-stats">
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val">{haloCold.decAr.median}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">Strix Halo, greedy AR</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">range {haloCold.decAr.min}–{haloCold.decAr.max}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val accent">{haloCold.decMtp.median}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">Strix Halo, greedy native MTP</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">{(haloCold.decMtp.median / haloCold.decAr.median).toFixed(2)}&times; AR &middot; range {haloCold.decMtp.min}–{haloCold.decMtp.max}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val accent">{haloCold.decSampledMtp.median}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">Strix Halo, sampled MTP with presence penalty 1.5</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">T 0.7 / top-p 0.8 / top-k 20 &middot; one disturbed repetition at {haloCold.decSampledMtp.min}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val">{r9700Fn.decAr.median}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">R9700, greedy AR</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">range {r9700Fn.decAr.min}–{r9700Fn.decAr.max}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val">{r9700Fn.decMtp.median}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">R9700, greedy native MTP</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">{(r9700Fn.decMtp.median / r9700Fn.decAr.median).toFixed(2)}&times; AR &middot; range {r9700Fn.decMtp.min}–{r9700Fn.decMtp.max}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val">{r9700Fn.decSampledMtp.median}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">R9700, sampled native MTP</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">range {r9700Fn.decSampledMtp.min}–{r9700Fn.decSampledMtp.max}</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lx-buys-foot">
          Penalties are about free on AR (Strix Halo 33.87 tok/s neutral vs 33.82
          penalized) and cost at most about 1.5&nbsp;% on sampled MTP (+0.74&nbsp;ms per
          52&nbsp;ms window). R9700 figures were measured on an earlier build of the
          0.4.1 release cycle and were not re-run on the final build. Model:
          <code>qwen3.8:flash-next</code>.
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The gap between the two cards is the row-cost table from
        &sect;&nbsp;05 in action: the same head, the same acceptance logic,
        and roughly twice the price per extra row on the R9700.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">09</span> Limits and switches</h2>
      <div class="lx-limit">
        <div class="lx-limit-title">what 0.4.1 does not claim</div>
        <ul class="distil-ul">
          <li>Sampled MTP preserves AR's sampling <em>distribution</em>, not its seeded token stream.</li>
          <li>With n-gram takeovers on, greedy output can differ from MTP-only output; it is not a universal greedy-token identity claim.</li>
          <li>The AR floor bounds speculative probing, not total request wall time.</li>
          <li>Cost-aware takeover pricing covers gfx1151 and gfx1201; other architectures keep the previous chooser.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>behaviour</th><th>default</th><th>opt-out</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>sampled native MTP</td><td>on</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_MTP_SAMPLED=0</code> (sampled requests run AR)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>n-gram takeovers inside MTP</td><td><code>auto</code>: on for Flash-Next on gfx1151 / gfx1201</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_MTP_NGRAM=0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>same-request AR floor</td><td>on</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_MTP_AR_FLOOR=0</code> (pre-floor chooser)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>GPU penalty prepass</td><td>on</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_PENALTY_PREPASS=0</code> (host penalties, same IDs)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">10</span> Sources</h2>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li><a href={tagBlob("crates/hipfire-arch-qwen4/src/mtp_spec.rs")}><code>crates/hipfire-arch-qwen4/src/mtp_spec.rs</code></a>: sampled verification (<code>SampledVerify</code>), penalty history, depth choice, row-cost fits, takeover pricing, AR floor.</li>
        <li><a href={tagBlob("crates/hipfire-generate/src/qwen.rs")}><code>crates/hipfire-generate/src/qwen.rs</code></a>: penalties installed on every MTP verify row.</li>
        <li><a href={tagBlob("crates/hipfire-config/src/lib.rs")}><code>crates/hipfire-config/src/lib.rs</code></a>: the 5/3/3 n-gram triple for Flash-Next on gfx1151 / gfx1201.</li>
        <li><a href={RELEASE_CHANGELOG}>CHANGELOG, v0.4.1</a> and the <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.1 release notes</a>: measured decode cells and penalty cost.</li>
        <li>Leviathan, Kalman, Matias, &ldquo;Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding&rdquo; (2023); Chen et&nbsp;al., &ldquo;Accelerating Large Language Model Decoding with Speculative Sampling&rdquo; (2023).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <LearnNav current="/learn/speculative-decoding" />
  </article>
</LearnLayout>
